  <div class="wrap">
<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout danger">A SECOND FISG-REFORMED PROVISION IN THIS SERIES: like §316a and §323's liability caps covered in the earlier Audit poster, §331a was directly reshaped by the 2021 FISG reform – spun out from its original home at §331 Nr.3a (introduced in 2007) into its own standalone offence, with penalties genuinely MORE severe than §331 itself.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">Not falsifying the numbers themselves – falsely swearing they're right</div>
      <div class="principle">§331, covered in the previous poster, criminalises misrepresenting the SUBSTANTIVE content of financial statements. §331a targets something genuinely different: falsely giving the SPECIFIC, FORMAL ASSURANCE (the "Bilanzeid," literally "balance sheet oath") that capital-market-oriented companies' legal representatives are required to sign, attesting that the accounts genuinely give a true and fair view. It is entirely possible for the underlying figures to be accurate while the FORMAL SIGNING PROCESS itself is compromised – and vice versa.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §331A MAPS TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Personal accountability at the point of sign-off</strong> – echoing §245's individual-accounts signature requirement established much earlier in this series, now given genuine criminal teeth specifically for the highest-stakes, capital-market-oriented population</span>
        <span><strong>Vertrauen (trust) as the protected legal interest</strong> – the confirmed "Schutzgesetz" status (Section 10) explicitly identifies trust in the accuracy of company information as what §331a exists to protect</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §331a covers, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>Element</span><span>Content</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>Conduct</span><span>Falsely giving the formal Bilanzeid assurance required under §264(2) s.3, §289(1) s.5, §297(2) s.4, §315(1) s.5/6</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>Täterkreis</span><span>Legal representatives of capital-market-oriented Kapitalgesellschaften specifically</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>Penalty</span><span>Up to 5 years (intent), 2 years (heightened negligence), or a €50,000 fine (ordinary negligence)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope – a Narrower Täterkreis Than §331</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Capital-market-oriented company representatives only</div>
      <div class="principle">Unlike §331, which binds management/supervisory board members across Kapitalgesellschaften generally, §331a's Täterkreis (circle of possible offenders) is confirmed to be considerably NARROWER – restricted specifically to the legal representatives (gesetzliche Vertreter) of a Kapitalgesellschaft that is capital-market-oriented per §2(14) WpHG (an "Inlandsemittent," domestic issuer). Genuinely a real Sonderdelikt: other persons involved in preparing the accounts – tax advisors, auditors – can only be liable as an Anstifter (instigator) or Gehilfe (aider/abettor), never as a principal offender, EVEN if they were themselves engaged to prepare the underlying statements.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>What the Bilanzeid Actually Is</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A formal, personal attestation layered on top of everything else this series has documented</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">The "Bilanzeid" is the formal, WRITTEN assurance capital-market-oriented companies' legal representatives must sign – per the specific cross-referenced provisions §264(2) sentence 3, §289(1) sentence 5, §297(2) sentence 4, and §315(1) sentences 5–6, all established across earlier posters in this series – personally attesting that, to the best of their knowledge, the Jahresabschluss/Konzernabschluss gives a true and fair view and the Lagebericht/Konzernlagebericht accurately describes the business's development, position, and material risks.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>History – From §331 Nr.3a (2007) to Standalone §331a (2021 FISG)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely traceable legislative history, culminating in the same reform covered earlier</div>
      <div class="principle">The Bilanzeid criminal offence was FIRST introduced in 2007 by the CSR-/Transparenzrichtlinie-Umsetzungsgesetz (TUG), housed at that time as §331 Nr.3a HGB – a single numbered point within §331 itself, rather than a standalone provision. The SAME 2021 FISG reform that reshaped §316a and §323's liability caps (covered in the earlier Audit poster) SPUN this content OUT into its own independent §331a, and CLARIFIED that its scope extends beyond the ordinary HGB accounts to cover IFRS-based accounts prepared for disclosure purposes too (Section 5).</div>
      <div class="callout suc">This is genuinely the SAME reform, appearing for the third time in this poster series – first shaping §316a's PIE definition and audit-firm rotation rules, then §323's tiered liability caps, and now §331a's standalone criminal offence. All three trace to the same 2021 Wirecard-driven legislative response.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Scope Clarification – HGB AND IFRS Disclosure-Purpose Accounts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A genuine touchpoint with IFRS group accounts covered earlier in this series</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">The 2021 FISG reform explicitly CLARIFIED that §331a covers not only the ordinary HGB Jahresabschluss/Konzernabschluss, but ALSO an IFRS-based individual or group statement prepared FOR DISCLOSURE PURPOSES. Recall from the earlier §315e poster: a capital-market-oriented parent's Konzernabschluss is generally REQUIRED to follow EU-IFRS rather than HGB's own §§290–314 framework – §331a's Bilanzeid obligation follows that SAME IFRS-based Konzernabschluss, meaning the false-assurance offence remains fully live even where the underlying financial statements themselves are IFRS, not HGB, documents.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example – Testing Who Falls Within the Täterkreis</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A genuinely narrower circle than the previous poster's §331 offence</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Three people involved in the same falsified accounts</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A listed AG's CFO (a legal representative/Vorstand member) signs a Bilanzeid he knows to be false. The company's external tax advisor, engaged to assist with preparing the underlying figures, was aware of the same underlying falsification but has no signing role of his own. The company's supervisory board chairman, who is NOT a legal representative of the company under German company law, also knew of the issue.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The CFO, as a legal representative signing the Bilanzeid itself, is the PRINCIPAL offender under §331a. The tax advisor, lacking the required status, can at most be liable as a Gehilfe (aider/abettor) – never as a principal §331a offender himself. The supervisory board chairman, also outside the defined Täterkreis for THIS specific offence, likewise cannot be a §331a principal, though he might separately face exposure under §331's own broader Nr.1/Nr.2 provisions covered in the previous poster, which DO reach supervisory board members.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>The Three-Tiered Penalty Structure – Genuinely More Severe Than §331</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A degree-of-fault structure genuinely similar in spirit to §323's own tiered liability caps</div>
      <div class="principle">§331a's penalty structure is confirmed to be genuinely THREE-TIERED by degree of fault – notably, its INTENT tier carries a MAXIMUM penalty of up to FIVE years' imprisonment, genuinely HARSHER than §331's own three-year maximum covered in the previous poster:</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th"><span>Degree of fault</span><span>Penalty</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>Vorsatz (intent) – §331a(1)</span><span>Imprisonment up to <strong>FIVE years</strong>, or a fine</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt"><span>Leichtfertigkeit (a heightened, "more than ordinary" negligence) – §331a(2)</span><span>Imprisonment up to <strong>TWO years</strong>, or a fine</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>"Einfache" (ordinary) Fahrlässigkeit</span><span><strong>NOT criminal</strong> – administrative fine up to €50,000 under §334(3) sentence 1</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">This three-tier structure – genuine criminal exposure for intent and heightened negligence, but only an administrative fine for ordinary negligence – is conceptually similar in SHAPE to §323's own degree-of-fault tiering for auditor liability covered in the earlier Audit poster, though the specific mechanism (criminal offence vs. civil liability cap) differs.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example – Applying Each of the Three Tiers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Identical false assurance, three genuinely different consequences depending on the mental state behind it</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Three CFOs signing the same false Bilanzeid, under different mental states</div>
        <div class="wk-body">Three different listed companies' CFOs each sign a Bilanzeid attesting to accounts that turn out to be materially wrong. CFO A knew with certainty the accounts were false and signed anyway. CFO B recklessly disregarded clear internal warning signs he should have investigated before signing. CFO C made a genuine, good-faith but ultimately careless review error, missing something a more diligent (but not grossly negligent) review would have caught.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">CFO A faces imprisonment of up to <strong>FIVE years</strong> under §331a(1) – genuine intent. CFO B faces imprisonment of up to <strong>TWO years</strong> under §331a(2) – Leichtfertigkeit, a recognisably heightened, reckless disregard falling short of full intent. CFO C faces only an <strong>administrative fine up to €50,000</strong> under §334(3) – ordinary negligence never crosses into criminal territory for this specific offence at all.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Why Both §331 and §331a Might Apply to the Same Underlying Conduct</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Two separate offences, potentially both committed by the same act</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Where a company's legal representative BOTH deliberately falsifies the underlying financial statements (§331) AND then signs the Bilanzeid falsely attesting those same statements are accurate (§331a), German criminal procedure may treat these as separately chargeable offences, reflecting the legislature's deliberate choice – following the 2007 and 2021 reforms – to create a LAYERED enforcement mechanism around both the underlying substantive falsification and the specific formal act of vouching for it.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>§331a as a Schutzgesetz – the Civil-Law Dimension</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A criminal offence that can also open the door to civil damages claims</div>
      <div class="principle">§331a is confirmed to qualify as a "Schutzgesetz" within the meaning of §823(2) of the German Civil Code (BGB) – a statute specifically designed to protect a defined class of persons, violation of which can independently support a CIVIL damages claim by an injured party, separate from any criminal prosecution. The protected legal interest is identified as the public's TRUST in the accuracy and completeness of information about the company – meaning an investor genuinely harmed by relying on a falsely-sworn Bilanzeid may have a civil damages route available specifically because §331a exists, even setting aside the criminal proceeding itself.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example – a Civil Damages Claim Riding on §331a</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A private investor's own path to compensation, alongside any criminal case</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: An investor relying on a falsely-sworn Bilanzeid</div>
        <div class="wk-body">An investor purchases shares in a listed company shortly after its CFO knowingly signs a false Bilanzeid, relying on the (falsely attested) financial statements in making the investment decision. The fraud is later discovered, and the share price collapses.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">SEPARATELY from any criminal proceeding against the CFO under §331a(1), the investor may pursue a CIVIL damages claim against the CFO (and potentially the company) under §823(2) BGB, using §331a's own "Schutzgesetz" status as the statutory hook – the investor does not need to wait for, or rely entirely on, the outcome of the criminal case to seek compensation for the loss the false assurance caused.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>An Honest Flag – Genuine Scope Uncertainty Remains</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A provision German legal scholarship itself acknowledges is not fully settled</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">German legal scholarship examining this offence (originally as §331 Nr.3a, now §331a) has GENUINELY, ACTIVELY debated its precise scope – particularly given that inaccurate accounting was ALREADY separately criminalised under §331 well before the Bilanzeid offence existed, raising real questions about exactly how much INDEPENDENT work §331a's own liability does beyond §331 itself. At least one specialised academic treatment on this exact question notes that a clarifying court decision remained outstanding as of its own writing. This poster presents the WELL-ESTABLISHED core structure (Täterkreis, penalty tiers, Schutzgesetz status) with genuine confidence, while flagging that some of the finer boundary questions remain a live, unsettled area of German legal scholarship rather than a fully closed question.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Distinguishing genuine Leichtfertigkeit (heightened, reckless negligence) from mere ordinary carelessness – the exact boundary carrying a THREE-YEAR imprisonment-exposure difference under this poster's own tiered structure</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>The genuinely unsettled scope questions flagged in Section 12, which practitioners must navigate without full judicial clarity on some boundary issues</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A criminal-law layer with no IFRS equivalent, applying even to IFRS-based accounts</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Precisely as with §331, IFRS itself has NO equivalent formal-assurance criminal offence – this remains entirely outside what an accounting standard could ever legislate. What makes §331a genuinely distinctive within this poster series, however, is that its scope EXPRESSLY reaches IFRS-based accounts prepared for disclosure purposes (Section 5) – meaning German criminal law's Bilanzeid enforcement mechanism follows the SUBSTANCE of a listed group's financial reporting obligation regardless of which accounting FRAMEWORK (HGB or, via §315e, EU-IFRS) actually governs the underlying figures.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §331a event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Bilanzeid falsely signed</span><span><span class="badge bo">Jahresabschluss/Konzernabschluss</span></span><span>Applies to HGB and disclosure-purpose IFRS accounts alike</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>§823(2) BGB civil claim</span><span>–</span><span>A separate, parallel civil-law consequence riding on the same false assurance</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Related Provisions – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §331a connects across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§264(2) s.3, §289(1) s.5, §297(2) s.4, §315(1) s.5-6</span><span>The underlying Bilanzeid requirements §331a's criminal offence protects</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§331</span><span>The closely related but genuinely distinct substantive-falsification offence covered in the previous poster</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§315e</span><span>The IFRS permission whose disclosure-purpose accounts §331a's scope clarification explicitly reaches</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§316a, §323</span><span>The other two FISG-reformed provisions covered in the earlier Audit poster, all tracing to the same 2021 legislative reform</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§334(3)</span><span>Supplies the administrative fine for ordinary negligence, where §331a's own criminal tiers don't reach</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Bilanzeid</div><div class="glossary-def">"Balance sheet oath" – the formal, personal written assurance capital-market-oriented companies' legal representatives must sign.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Inlandsemittent</div><div class="glossary-def">Domestic issuer – a company issuing shares or debt securities, per §2(14) WpHG, defining §331a's specific Täterkreis.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Leichtfertigkeit</div><div class="glossary-def">A heightened, "more than ordinary" degree of negligence – carrying criminal exposure under §331a(2), unlike ordinary Fahrlässigkeit.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">FISG</div><div class="glossary-def">The 2021 Finanzmarktintegritätsstärkungsgesetz, which spun the Bilanzeid offence out of §331 Nr.3a into standalone §331a.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Schutzgesetz</div><div class="glossary-def">A protective statute under §823(2) BGB, violation of which independently supports a civil damages claim.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Gehilfe / Anstifter</div><div class="glossary-def">Aider/abettor or instigator – the only roles available to someone outside §331a's own narrow Täterkreis.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bo">Jahresabschluss/Konzernabschluss</span> The accounts the Bilanzeid attests to
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
